    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Geraldo Perdomo** (72.67) and **Cal Raleigh** (72.64) anchor the lineup as our most potent options. **Geraldo Perdomo** benefits from an elite matchup against Jeffrey Springs, with his projected #2 spot in the order driving a significant +8% efficiency boost. Similarly, **Cal Raleigh** is poised for a high-output day against the southpaw Andrew Abbott; despite potential park headwinds, his +19% SP skill advantage and platoon-favorable profile make him a must-start. **Daulton Varsho** remains a Tier 1 play at 71.62, leveraging a massive +19% SP skill advantage against N. Martinez, and as our primary speed threat, his dynamic platoon edge (+10%) is essential for keeping our stolen base totals competitive.\n\nIn the mid-tier, **Taylor Ward** offers excellent value at 64.85, bolstered by a +10% leadoff order premium against Tolle, while **Luis Arraez** (64.37) continues to be the bedrock of our batting average floor. The most difficult bench decision involved **Jonathan Aranda**, whose 52.24 score hovered just outside the starting threshold. While **Jonathan Aranda** benefits from a strong platoon split against Cease, his efficiency was capped by the opposing pitcher's high strikeout profile (SP Skill: -19%), ultimately pushing him behind **Gavin Sheets** and **Tyler Freeman** in the optimization hierarchy. \n\nManagers should keep a close eye on the late-breaking lineups for **Ryan Waldschmidt**, **Lawrence Butler**, and **Cal Raleigh**; the optimizer has factored in historical assumptions, but confirmed batting orders are critical for maintaining these scoring efficiencies. **Lawrence Butler** is a high-variance start today; while his matchup against the soft-tossing Mitch Bratt (7.04 xERA) is enticing, his -13% platoon penalty remains a significant drag on his overall profile. Proceed with caution on **Jeremiah Jackson** (46.16) as he faces the narrowest margin for error in the lineup, functioning as a purely marginal start given the unfavorable hitting environment.",
